        "content_section_R4q73x": {
          "type": "content_section",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why Investment Jewelry Beats Fashion Jewelry Every Time",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "heading_font_size": 48,
            "subheading": "",
            "subheading_text_color": "#8b7d4a",
            "subheading_background": "#f5f2eb",
            "rich_text": "<p>Fashion jewelry is designed to be current. Investment jewelry is designed to be permanent. The difference shows up in the materials: solid 14K or 18K gold versus gold-plated brass, lab or natural diamonds versus cubic zirconia. It also shows up in what happens five years later investment pieces look identical, fashion pieces have faded, tarnished, or dated themselves.<br/><br/>14K gold is the standard for everyday fine jewelry: durable enough to withstand daily wear, pure enough to hold its value, and available in yellow, white, and rose. Lab diamonds are chemically and optically identical to mined diamonds — a gemologist cannot tell them apart with a loupe. They offer the same brilliance at better value, with cleaner provenance. For a piece you intend to wear every day for thirty years, these are the right materials.</p><p> By 30, the case for investment jewelry is simple: you've outgrown the trend cycle, you know what you actually wear, and you'd rather own six things that work than sixty that don't.<br/><br/></p>",
            "products_heading": "Featured pieces:",
