        "content_section_bXjKmT": {
          "type": "content_section",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How to Build Your Fine Jewelry Collection by 30",
            "heading_tag": "h3",
            "heading_font_size": 36,
            "subheading": "",
            "subheading_text_color": "#8b7d4a",
            "subheading_background": "#f5f2eb",
            "rich_text": "<p>You don't need to acquire all six pieces at once. The point of investment jewelry is that each piece earns its place through daily use — which means you should start with the one you'd actually reach for every morning and build from there.</p><p>Start with diamond studs or gold hoops — whichever you wear more. Add the necklace when you find yourself reaching for one. The tennis bracelet and band ring are the pieces that reward you later, when you realise you've been wearing them for ten years without thinking about it. And the signature piece can come at any point — it doesn't follow a sequence, it follows a feeling.</p><p>Six pieces. Worn every day. Still working at 60. That's the brief — and that's investment jewelry done right.</p>",
            "products_heading": "Featured pieces:",
            "product_list": [],
            "accent_text": "",
            "accent_text_color": "",
            "button_label": "",
            "button_link": "",
            "button_style": "primary"
          }
        },
        "faq_main": {
          "type": "faq",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Fine Jewelry FAQ",
            "intro": "<p><strong>What jewelry should every woman own?</strong></p><p>The essential jewelry pieces every woman should own are: a pair of diamond studs, solid gold hoops, a diamond necklace (station or tennis style), a tennis bracelet, a diamond band ring for the right hand, and one personal signature piece in a gemstone or style that's uniquely hers. These six pieces cover every occasion from a morning meeting to a black-tie event without requiring additional pieces.</p><p><strong>What is the most important piece of jewelry to own?</strong></p><p>Diamond studs are widely considered the most essential piece of jewelry a woman can own. They are the most versatile — appropriate for every context from casual to formal — and the longest-lasting in terms of style relevance. A well-made pair of diamond studs in solid gold looks as current today as it did thirty years ago, and will look the same thirty years from now.</p><p><strong>Is it worth buying fine jewelry in your 30s?</strong></p><p>Yes, and your 30s are the ideal time to invest in fine jewelry. By this point, most women have a clearer sense of what they actually wear, which means fewer wasted purchases. Fine jewelry in solid gold with real diamonds holds its appearance indefinitely — unlike fashion jewelry, which tarnishes and dates. One well-chosen investment piece in your 30s will outlast many seasons' worth of trend purchases.</p><p><strong>What is the difference between fine jewelry and fashion jewelry?</strong></p><p>Fine jewelry is made from precious metals (solid 14K or 18K gold, sterling silver, platinum) and genuine stones (natural or lab diamonds, sapphires, rubies, emeralds). Fashion jewelry is typically made from base metals with gold or silver plating, and synthetic or simulated stones. Fine jewelry maintains its appearance indefinitely with proper care; fashion jewelry tarnishes, fades, and dates itself. The price difference is a one-time premium for pieces that last a lifetime.</p><p><strong>Are lab diamonds a good choice for investment jewelry?</strong></p><p>Lab diamonds are an excellent choice for investment jewelry pieces intended for daily wear. They are chemically, optically, and physically identical to mined diamonds — indistinguishable even to a gemologist with a loupe. For pieces like studs, tennis bracelets, and band rings that you wear every day, lab diamonds offer the same brilliance and durability as mined at a better value, allowing you to prioritise setting quality and metal weight.</p><p><strong>What is the best first fine jewelry piece to buy?</strong></p><p>Diamond studs are the best first fine jewelry purchase for most women. They are the most versatile piece in any collection — appropriate for every occasion, every outfit, and every age — and they require no coordination with other pieces. A pair of classic round diamond studs in 14K gold, in a size between 0.5ct and 1ct total weight, is a purchase you will not second-guess.</p><p><strong>What makes 14K gold better than 18K for everyday jewelry?</strong></p><p>14K gold contains 58.3% pure gold alloyed with metals like silver and copper, while 18K gold is 75% pure. The alloy in 14K gold makes it significantly more durable and scratch-resistant — better suited to pieces worn daily like rings, bracelets, and hoops that experience regular friction. 18K gold is softer and more prone to surface wear over time. For investment jewelry worn every day, 14K gold is the more practical choice, while 18K is better reserved for special occasion pieces worn less frequently.</p><p><strong>How do you wear a tennis bracelet every day?</strong></p><p>A tennis bracelet worn as an everyday piece should be worn slightly loose — loose enough to move freely on the wrist, not so loose that it slides over the hand. Wear it alone on one wrist for maximum impact; the old money approach is one wrist, one bracelet, nothing else. It pairs naturally with a watch on the opposite wrist. The classic and oval bezel settings both work well for daily wear, with the bezel setting sitting slightly lower profile against the wrist.</p><h2></h2>",
            "background_color": "#f0f0f0",
            "border_top_color": "#ecd597"
          }
        },
